{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high vs. realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward. Apply a downward level shift (larger for longer horizons).",
    "Downtrend underestimation (insufficient bearishness): in sustained sell-offs the model declines too slowly and predicts rebounds that do not materialize. Increase negative drift when recent 10–20d momentum is negative.",
    "Rebound/mean-reversion overshoot: after sharp drops the model expects stronger mean reversion than observed, leading to overprediction around local troughs. Damp mean-reversion strength when volatility is elevated and the last 5–10d trend is down.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: forecast error widens with horizon (small early, larger late). Use a horizon-scaled correction where the downward adjustment increases with lead time.",
    "Peak/mini-rally overshoot: short-lived upticks in the model (local peaks around days ~12–18) are higher and longer than realized. Smooth/dampen forecast oscillations by reducing amplitude of predicted rallies during broader downtrends."
  ]
}
